WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.835
Local AI workflows may use large language models, embedding models, vision models,

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:06.635
speech-to-text, and text-to-speech.

00:00:06.635 --> 00:00:08.940
Each model category supports different tasks.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.280
A language model may help draft, summarize, classify, or reason over text.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.080
Embeddings help represent documents for search and retrieval.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.360
Vision models can classify images or extract visual information.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.880
Speech models support transcription and generated narration.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.185
A useful lab often combines several model types rather than relying on one model for

00:00:26.185 --> 00:00:26.966
everything.

00:00:26.966 --> 00:00:30.920
Model size, context length, quantization, licensing, and hardware fit all matter.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.680
A larger model may perform better on some tasks but require more memory and power.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.920
A longer context window may help with larger documents but increase resource needs.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:43.075
A permissive license may support experimentation that a restricted license does not.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:47.040
Privacy requirements may determine whether a model is appropriate for certain data.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.680
Model selection should be based on the task, not only leaderboard popularity.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.726
Inference services make models available to applications through local APIs or web

00:00:54.726 --> 00:00:55.119
frontends.

00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:59.710
This can turn a one-off model into a reusable lab service for notebooks, automation,

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.640
chat interfaces, and prototypes.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.405
Serving introduces operational questions: what port is exposed, who can reach it,

00:01:05.405 --> 00:01:09.560
whether authentication is needed, how concurrency is handled, and how logs are stored.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.320
A model server is software infrastructure, even when it runs on a personal machine.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.120
Batching and concurrency should be understood at a high level.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.910
Running multiple requests at once can improve utilization, but it may increase latency,

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.840
memory pressure, or failure rates.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.520
A small homelab may not need complex scaling, but it does need realistic expectations.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.220
If a model is slow, the answer might be a smaller model, a different quantization,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.720
a shorter context, better hardware, or a narrower workflow.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.560
Local inference works best when models are matched to the actual job.
